## 1. GGUF reader: guard against `n_tensors` / `n_kv` values that would exhaust memory before the bounds-checked cursor can refuse

**Component.** `include/strata/artifact/gguf_reader.hpp` — `GgufFile::parse()`.

**Responsibilities.** mmap a GGUF v3 file, parse the header, metadata KV and tensor directory with a bounds-checked `Cursor`, refuse duplicate tensor names, and compute the aligned data start.

**Problem.** `tensors_.reserve((size_t)n_tensors)` and the loop bound `n_tensors`/`n_kv` are read straight from the file before any structural check. A corrupt or hostile file declaring `n_tensors = 2^63` makes `reserve` throw `std::length_error`/`bad_alloc` (or attempt a multi-terabyte allocation) *before* the cursor's EOF check would have refused the file. The cursor protects reads, not the pre-read count. The same applies to `MetaType::ARRAY`, which is guarded (`1u << 24`) — the header counts are the unguarded siblings.

**Change.**

```diff
--- a/include/strata/artifact/gguf_reader.hpp
+++ b/include/strata/artifact/gguf_reader.hpp
@@
     void parse() {
         Cursor c(base_, size_);
         const uint32_t magic = c.read<uint32_t>();
         if (magic != 0x46554747u) throw std::runtime_error("not a GGUF file (bad magic)");
         version_ = c.read<uint32_t>();
         if (version_ != 3)
             throw std::runtime_error("GGUF v" + std::to_string(version_) + ", this reader handles v3");
         const uint64_t n_tensors = c.read<uint64_t>();
         const uint64_t n_kv = c.read<uint64_t>();
+        // The counts are the file's own claim, and reserve()/the loop trust them BEFORE the cursor can
+        // see the EOF that refutes them: a header declaring 2^60 tensors throws bad_alloc from reserve
+        // instead of the precise "unexpected end of file" the cursor would give.  A tensor entry is at
+        // least 4 (name length) + 4 (n_dims) + 4 (type) + 8 (offset) = 20 bytes, so a count that cannot
+        // fit the remaining header bytes is refused here, at the same point the cursor would refuse a
+        // short read - and reserve() then only ever sees a number the file can back.
+        const size_t header_left = size_ - c.pos();
+        if (n_tensors > header_left / 20)
+            throw std::runtime_error("GGUF: header declares " + std::to_string(n_tensors) +
+                                     " tensors, which cannot fit the " + std::to_string(header_left) +
+                                     " bytes after it");
+        if (n_kv > header_left / 9)   // a KV entry is at least 8 (key length) + 1 (type)
+            throw std::runtime_error("GGUF: header declares " + std::to_string(n_kv) +
+                                     " metadata keys, which cannot fit the " + std::to_string(header_left) +
+                                     " bytes after it");

         for (uint64_t i = 0; i < n_kv; ++i) {
```

**Verification.**
1. *Synthetic corrupt header (extends `src/artifact/gguf_reader_test.cpp`):* copy the tiny model's header, rewrite `n_tensors` to `0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF`, and assert `GgufFile` throws with a message containing "cannot fit" — not `bad_alloc`, and not a crash. Run under a memory-limited CI job to prove no large allocation is attempted.
2. *Regression:* the existing `--check` mode over the real shards still reports 1,223 / 1 tensors and 202 Q2_0 in shard 1, and `gguf_split_test` still passes (its synthetic shards declare small counts, so the new guard is inert for them).

## 2. GGUF reader: `tensor_payload_bytes` silently returns 0 for unknown types, so `GgufModel::in_bounds` cannot distinguish "unknown" from "empty"

**Component.** `include/strata/artifact/gguf_reader.hpp` — `tensor_payload_bytes()` and `GgufModel::in_bounds()`.

**Responsibilities.** Compute a tensor's byte payload from shape and block geometry, and answer whether a tensor lies inside its shard file.

**Problem.** `tensor_payload_bytes` returns 0 for an unknown type, a non-whole-block row, or an overflow. `in_bounds` treats 0 as "not in bounds", which is correct for refusal, but the caller cannot tell a genuinely empty tensor from an unsupported encoding — and the engine's refusal messages ("the engine checks every layer's formats at start and refuses an unsupported one by name", docs/UNSLOTH_Q4.md) need the *reason*, not a boolean. Today the reason is lost at this boundary and re-derived (differently) downstream.

**Change.**

```diff
--- a/include/strata/artifact/gguf_reader.hpp
+++ b/include/strata/artifact/gguf_reader.hpp
@@
 // Bytes of a tensor's payload from its shape and block geometry; 0 when the type is unknown, a row is not whole
 // blocks, or the count overflows.
-inline uint64_t tensor_payload_bytes(const TensorInfo& t) {
+// `why`, when non-null, receives the reason a 0 was returned ("" when the size is valid), so a loader can refuse
+// an unsupported encoding BY NAME instead of reporting "out of bounds" for a tensor that is merely unknown.
+inline uint64_t tensor_payload_bytes(const TensorInfo& t, std::string* why = nullptr) {
     int be = 0, bb = 0;
-    if (t.shape.empty() || !block_geometry(t.type, be, bb) || t.shape[0] % (uint64_t) be) return 0;
+    if (t.shape.empty()) { if (why) *why = "empty shape"; return 0; }
+    if (!block_geometry(t.type, be, bb)) {
+        if (why) *why = std::string("unsupported type ") + ggml_type_name(t.type) + " (id " + std::to_string(t.type) + ")";
+        return 0;
+    }
+    if (t.shape[0] % (uint64_t) be) {
+        if (why) *why = "row width " + std::to_string(t.shape[0]) + " is not a multiple of block " + std::to_string(be);
+        return 0;
+    }
     uint64_t elements = 1;
     for (uint64_t d : t.shape) {
-        if (d == 0 || elements > (std::numeric_limits<uint64_t>::max)() / d) return 0;
+        if (d == 0 || elements > (std::numeric_limits<uint64_t>::max)() / d) {
+            if (why) *why = "element count overflow";
+            return 0;
+        }
         elements *= d;
     }
     const uint64_t blocks = elements / (uint64_t) be;
-    if (blocks > (std::numeric_limits<uint64_t>::max)() / (uint64_t) bb) return 0;
+    if (blocks > (std::numeric_limits<uint64_t>::max)() / (uint64_t) bb) {
+        if (why) *why = "byte count overflow";
+        return 0;
+    }
     return blocks * (uint64_t) bb;
 }
@@
     /// Whether `t` (a tensor of shard `s`) has a known byte count that lies inside its file.
-    bool in_bounds(const TensorInfo& t, size_t s) const {
+    /// `why` (optional) carries why it is not, distinguishing an unsupported type from a truncated file.
+    bool in_bounds(const TensorInfo& t, size_t s, std::string* why = nullptr) const {
         const GgufFile& g = *shards_[s];
-        const uint64_t bytes = tensor_payload_bytes(t);
+        const uint64_t bytes = tensor_payload_bytes(t, why);
+        if (bytes == 0) return false;
         const uint64_t payload = g.file_size() - g.data_start();
-        return bytes != 0 && t.offset <= payload && bytes <= payload - t.offset;
+        if (t.offset > payload || bytes > payload - t.offset) {
+            if (why) *why = "tensor needs " + std::to_string(bytes) + " bytes at offset " + std::to_string(t.offset) +
+                            ", the shard has " + std::to_string(payload) + " of payload";
+            return false;
+        }
+        return true;
     }
```

**Verification.**
1. *Unit:* a `TensorInfo` with `type = 99` and `why` set must return 0 with `why` containing "unsupported type ? (id 99)"; a Q2_0 tensor with `shape[0] = 63` must say "not a multiple of block 64".
2. *Integration:* `gguf_split_test`'s truncated-shard case must now report "the shard has N of payload" rather than a bare refusal, and the existing duplicate-name and split-key refusals must be unchanged (byte-identical messages).

## 3. `gguf_split_paths`: the fixed 5+5 digit layout rejects legitimate llama.cpp split names and mis-detects a `.gguf` suffix check

**Component.** `include/strata/artifact/gguf_split.hpp` — `gguf_split_paths()`.

**Responsibilities.** Given any shard path, return every shard of the split model in order, throwing when one is missing.

**Problem.** The detector requires `any.size() == at + 4 + 5 + 5` and `any.compare(at + 9, 5, ".gguf") == 0`. That hard-codes exactly five digits after `-of-` *and* a bare `.gguf` extension. A file named `model-00001-of-00002.GGUF` (Windows case-insensitive filesystems, HF mirrors) or with any trailing qualifier is silently treated as a one-shard model, so the engine opens shard 1 alone — the exact failure mode the header's own comment says was fixed ("a download that was still running … produced a model with some layers missing"). The digit-count requirement is fine (llama.cpp always writes `%05d`), but the extension comparison is a needless second failure surface.

**Change.**

```diff
--- a/include/strata/artifact/gguf_split.hpp
+++ b/include/strata/artifact/gguf_split.hpp
@@
     static const char tag[] = "-of-";
     const size_t at = any.rfind(tag);
     // "...-NNNNN-of-MMMMM.gguf": five digits before the tag, five after it, then the extension
-    if (at == std::string::npos || at < 6 || any[at - 6] != '-' || any.size() != at + 4 + 5 + 5 ||
-        any.compare(at + 9, 5, ".gguf") != 0)
+    // The extension is matched case-insensitively: a Windows or mirror-hosted "....GGUF" is the same file, and
+    // treating it as a one-shard model opens shard 1 alone and loses every layer that lives in the others -
+    // the silent partial model this function exists to prevent.  The five-digit fields stay strict: llama.cpp
+    // always writes %05d, and a looser pattern would match names that are not split files at all.
+    if (at == std::string::npos || at < 6 || any[at - 6] != '-' || any.size() < at + 4 + 5 + 5)
         return {any};
+    const size_t digits_end = at + 4 + 5;
+    const std::string ext = any.substr(digits_end);
+    if (ext.size() != 5) return {any};
+    for (size_t i = 0; i < 5; ++i)
+        if (std::tolower((unsigned char) ext[i]) != ".gguf"[i]) return {any};
     int no = 0, n = 0;
     for (size_t i = 0; i < 5; ++i) {
         const char a = any[at - 5 + i], b = any[at + 4 + i];
         if (a < '0' || a > '9' || b < '0' || b > '9') return {any};
         no = no * 10 + (a - '0');
         n = n * 10 + (b - '0');
     }
     if (n < 1 || no < 1 || no > n) return {any};
     std::vector<std::string> out;
     for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {
         char num[8];
         std::snprintf(num, sizeof num, "%05d", i);
         std::string p = any;
         p.replace(at - 5, 5, num);
         if (!std::ifstream(p, std::ios::binary))
             throw std::runtime_error("missing model shard " + p + " (shard " + std::to_string(i) + " of " +
                                      std::to_string(n) + "; is the download complete?)");
         out.push_back(std::move(p));
     }
     return out;
```

Add `#include <cctype>` to the header's includes.

